> It went through the door on the third try. Haruta called the angles, calmly, and was right about the last one.
> So now the worst orange sofa in Seoul is on the pavement outside the laundromat, in the first sun. It looks like it's been waiting all its life to be outdoors.
> Songha is pacing up and down with his phone, booking a van. Daniel has his eyes closed along one arm of the sofa. Charlie is on the kerb with two coffees, reading the laundromat's opening hours.
@show hanbi smile at=c
> You and Hanbi have the middle. You've put your bag down on the ground, and you haven't kept hold of the strap.
> He unfolds the old portrait on his knee, the blank oval upward, and takes the pencil from behind his ear. It's the one you gave back.
hanbi: …I know now. How to draw you.
you: Since when?
hanbi: …Since you put your bag down.
hanbi: I said it when we were sixteen. You looked like you were waiting. I didn't know for what. So I couldn't do the face.
you: And now you know.
hanbi: …Somewhere to put your things. That's all. It isn't much to want. You've wanted it a long time.
> He says it kindly, as a fact, the way he'd tell you your phone was at four percent. It goes through you like warm water.
hanbi: …Hold still. Thirty seconds. I won't be kind.
@sfx pencil
> You hold still. The pencil doesn't leave the page. Across the alley the laundromat's dryers turn. Behind you, Songha confirms a van.
> Then he turns the portrait round.
> It's you, at sixteen and tonight, both at once. He hasn't made it kind, and it isn't flattering. It's a person who has been told they can stay, and who's beginning to believe it.
you: …That's what I look like?
hanbi[smile]: …That's what you look like when you're not waiting.
* [sincere] "I don't have a word for what you are to me. I'd like one."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi[thinking]: …Family. The kind you choose. I've got seven already.
    hanbi[smile]: …There was always a space at the end of the sofa. I thought I'd drawn it wrong.
* [playful] "Six years, and you did it in thirty seconds."
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    hanbi[laugh]: …Six years and thirty seconds. Don't round it down.
    hanbi: …I needed all of it. I needed to know who I was drawing. You're family. That's who.
* [quiet] Lean your shoulder against his, and look at the drawing together.
    @rel hanbi closeness+1
    > He leans back, exactly as much. You hold each other up like two books on a shelf.
    hanbi: …This is what I meant. When I said a place to rest. It's this. It's family. I get one too.
> He opens the sketchbook to the inside of the front cover. There's a line there in his sixteen-year-old capitals: *IF FOUND, RETURN TO: HANBI.*
> Underneath it, pressing hard, he writes: *OR {{playerName}}.*
you: What's that for?
hanbi: …In case I lose it. It should know where to go.
hanbi[smile]: …And in case you lose track. Of where to go. It's written down now. Writing stays.
> You've had four addresses, and none of them ever had you on somebody else's cover. You read the line twice, like a word you're learning to spell.
> You take the sharpener out of your coat pocket. *Put it somewhere you're staying. It needs to be clamped to something.*
> The sofa has a wooden rail along the back. You fit the clamp over it and tighten the screw with a hundred-won coin.
hanbi[thinking]: …There?
you: It goes where the sofa goes. The sofa goes where you all go. I've decided I'm easy to find.
> He looks at the sharpener for a long moment. It's a grey machine bolted to a terrible sofa in the morning sun. He is plainly, quietly happy, and he doesn't hide it.
hanbi[smile]: …Then I'll always have a sharp pencil. And you'll always have to come and use it.
you: That was the plan.
hanbi: …It's a good plan. It doesn't have an end date. I checked.
@show hyunjun smile at=r
hyunjun: Breakfast. I'm calling it, and I'm buying. All of us. The sofa can hold the table.
@show songha smile at=l
songha: The sofa is staying exactly where it is. The van's at nine. Hanbi, I need a charger. I'm at two percent, and I've got a van to manage.
> Hanbi reaches for his cardigan pocket, and stops. You're already holding it out to Songha. It's still wound in his neat figure of eight.
songha[smile]: …Ah. There are two of you now.
hanbi[laugh]: …There are two of us now.
@hide all
> He hands you a bottle of water from somewhere. You drink half and hand it back, and he drinks the rest without being asked.
> *I hope, by the time you read this, you've found a place you want to stay.*
> You have. It's orange, it's on a pavement, and it leaves in a van at nine. You know exactly where it's going.
> You stay on it, next to him, until then.
@ending hanbi_beside
