> There's a bench outside the laundromat, under the sign that's always on. Behind the glass one dryer is still turning. It's somebody's forgotten towels, going round and round.
@show hanbi neutral at=c
> You sit. He sits. The alley is peach and grey and very quiet. The STUDIO 25 sign is across the way, with its crooked 2, the right way round again.
> He has his hands round the folded *FUTURE LETTER* card. He turns it over once, and puts it down on the bench between you.
@if hanbi.romance >= 4
    hanbi: …I said it in the drawn room. I'd like to say it again. Out here, where the rain's real.
    hanbi: …I like you.
@else
    hanbi: …In the drawn room I said I wanted to see you again. I didn't know what it was yet. I've found the rest of the sentence since.
    hanbi: …I like you.
> There's no dash. He looks at you the whole way through it.
you: I like you too.
> It's out, in ordinary daylight, on a bench. The dryer goes on turning. Nothing breaks. It's the easiest true thing you've said in years.
hanbi[smile]: …Good. I hoped you would.
hanbi: …I'd thought about what to say if you said it back. It turns out I'd rather just look at you for a minute.
* [sincere] "I don't want it to be something that happened because a building was closing."
    @rel hanbi trust+1
    hanbi: …No. Me neither. I want it to happen on an ordinary day. One we chose.
* [playful] "We've been awake for twenty-two hours. I'd like to like you on some sleep."
